// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`the story under the description's rules (decision 4) > the prompt carries the roaster voice with attribution, never the old descriptor list (snapshot) 1`] = `
"Write a 120 to 200 word story for "Lighter" covering: where it's from (region-level only, e.g. "the highlands of X", never a specific farm, co-op, lot, estate, or importer name), how it's processed (in plain language), what that does to the cup (tied to the structure below), and one thing worth noticing.

Origin reference (distill to region-level ONLY; do not repeat any farm, co-op, lot, or estate name verbatim even if present here): Finca Example, Huila, Colombia
Process reference: Washed

The roaster's signature notes (the core of the cup, strongest first): Dark Chocolate, Hazelnut
The roaster's accent notes (only ever "a hint of"): Cherry
Of the roaster's notes, Axis & Bloom's cupping table tasted these too: Dark Chocolate

Structure on a 0 to 15 scale (the labels are the two extremes; a middle value is moderate):
  Body: 8 to 10 out of 15 (scale from thin to heavy)

Rules:
- Never name a specific farm, co-op, cooperative, estate, lot, or importer.
- Never name the roaster or any roastery ("the roaster" is how you refer to them).
- Refer to it only as "Lighter" or "this coffee", never the internal catalog name, and never invent a different name or nickname of your own for it.
- Flavor words: only the roaster's notes listed above, never one of your own. Attribute them to the roaster ("the roaster calls it", "per the roaster"). Where our cupping table tasted one of them too you may add "and we tasted it too"; never present a tasting as Axis & Bloom's own ("our cupping revealed", "we found", "our cuppers noticed" are not allowed).
- Structure (body, sweetness, acidity, bitterness, finish) may be described plainly, in proportion to the scale; never exaggerate a middle value.
- Never use the word "archetype", and never put a category word such as chocolate & nutty, fruity in front of "coffee", "blend", "cup", "style" or "profile".
- Say nothing about adding milk or drinking it black (a note's own name, such as milk chocolate, is fine).
- Never use an em dash or an en dash. Use commas, periods or colons instead.
- Plain prose only. No title, no heading, no markdown formatting (no "#", no bold); start directly with the first sentence of the story.
- Warm, specific, editorial voice. No marketing language.
- 120 to 200 words."
`;
